@node Main "amifleet68"

@{b}amifleet68 0.3.0@{ub} - the amiagent fleet console, native on the Amiga

Watch every Amiga running amiagent from one window, run AmigaDOS commands
on them, move files, see and drive their screens, and connect to AmiVNC.
amifleet68 speaks the amimcp wire protocol straight over TCP. There is no
server to install and no PC or Mac in the loop.

    @{" Requirements     " link Requirements}  what it needs
    @{" Getting started  " link Start}  the first run, adding machines
    @{" The fleet board  " link Board}  what the columns mean
    @{" Details window   " link Details}  report, shell, files
    @{" Screen window    " link Screen}  the machine's screen, through the agent
    @{" VNC window       " link VNC}  a session to AmiVNC
    @{" ARexx            " link ARexx}  the AMIFLEET.1 port
    @{" Tips and limits  " link Tips}  what is worth knowing
    @{" Security         " link Security}  read this before you open ports
    @{" History          " link History}
    @{" Author & licence " link Author}

@endnode

@node Requirements "Requirements"

@{b}On the Amiga running amifleet68@{ub}

  - AmigaOS 3.0 or later (3.1 for the planar Screen/VNC drawing).
    amifleet68 runs on a plain 68000; amifleet68.020 is for 68020+.
  - MUI 3.8 or later (muimaster.library v19). With amipkg:
    amipkg install mui38
  - A running TCP/IP stack: Roadshow, AmiTCP, Miami (bsdsocket.library v4).
  - Optional: cybergraphics.library (Picasso96 or CyberGraphX) for fast
    truecolour drawing of remote screens.

@{b}On every machine you want to see@{ub}

  - amiagent 0.5.0 or later (0.13.0 recommended), started with a TOKEN.
    Aminet: comm/net/amiagent, or  amipkg install amiagent
  - For VNC: AmiVNC (Aminet comm/tcp/AmiVNC.lha, or  amipkg install amivnc)
    in SYS:Programs/AmiVNC/. amifleet68 starts it for you.

@endnode

@node Start "Getting started"

Double-click the amifleet68 icon, or run it from a Shell.

The first run shows one machine, @{i}This Amiga@{ui}, at 127.0.0.1: the
agent on the machine you are sitting at. It will say @{b}token?@{ub} until you
select it, click @{b}Edit...@{ub} and enter the TOKEN your agent was started
with.

To add other machines:

  @{b}Scan network...@{ub}  sweeps x.y.z.1-254 for amiagent on port 7846 and adds
                    every agent it finds. The network is filled in from
                    this Amiga's own address. Takes about 25 seconds.

  @{b}Add...@{ub}           name, host (name or address), port, token.

The list is saved in ENV: and ENVARC:amifleet68.prefs, one machine per line:
name, host, port and token, separated by TABs.

@endnode

@node Board "The fleet board"

One row per machine, refreshed every five seconds:

  @{b}State@{ub}   online, @{i}offline@{ui}, @{i}token?@{ui} (the agent refused our token),
          or @{b}busy@{ub} while one of your commands runs there.
  @{b}Ping@{ub}    how long the last report took, round trip.
  @{b}Agent@{ub}   amiagent version.   @{b}CPU@{ub}, @{b}OS@{ub}   as the machine reports them.
  @{b}Chip@{ub}, @{b}Fast@{ub}   free memory.

A machine that drops off keeps its last report, in italics. It is only
marked offline after two missed reports: the agent serves one connection
at a time, so a single miss usually just means someone else was talking
to it.

Double-click a row for its @{" Details " link Details}. The toolbar buttons
work on the selected row. Hold the pointer over a button for a hint.

@endnode

@node Details "Details window"

@{b}Info@{ub}   the machine's full report: agent, Kickstart, CPU, FPU, memory,
       mounted volumes, current directory.

@{b}Shell@{ub}  type an AmigaDOS command and press Return. It runs on that machine
       and the output comes back with the return code and the time taken.

       The agent waits 20 seconds for a command. After that it answers
       "still running" and gets on with other work, and @{b}Break@{ub} can send
       the command Ctrl-C. (While it waits, the agent answers nothing
       else - that is why the board shows @{b}busy@{ub}.)

@{b}Files@{ub}  browse that machine's disks. Type a path and press Return,
       double-click a drawer to open it, @{b}Parent@{ub} to go up.

       @{b}Download...@{ub} saves the selected file on this Amiga. Large files
       come in 64 KB pieces, so memory is no limit.
       @{b}Upload...@{ub} sends a file from this Amiga into the drawer shown
       (up to 16 MB).

@endnode

@node Screen "Screen window"

Shows the machine's front screen, grabbed by the agent itself. It works on
any screen: RTG, planar, a game, even a Guru.

With @{b}Live@{ub} on, amifleet68 asks for a cheap checksum once a second and
fetches the full picture only when it changed. A big truecolour screen
takes a few seconds to fetch; the status line says how long.

Click on the picture to click on that machine. Drag to drag. Type while the
pointer is over the picture and the keys go to that machine, through its
own keymap.

Watching your own screen works, but every fetch changes the screen you are
watching, so it never settles.

@endnode

@node VNC "VNC window"

A VNC session to @{b}AmiVNC@{ub} on the selected machine.

@{b}Connect@{ub} first tries the VNC port. If nothing answers, amifleet68 asks
the agent to start AmiVNC (it sets the password and runs it in the
background) and connects when it is up.

  @{b}Fast (8-bit)@{ub}  AmiVNC's 256-colour mode on port 5901: a quarter of
                the data. On by default. Off: full colour on port 5900.
  @{b}Password@{ub}      default "amiga". AmiVNC keeps 7 characters at most.

The mouse (with movement) and the keyboard go to the remote machine while
the pointer is over the picture.

amifleet68 asks for at most ten updates a second. The first version asked
as fast as AmiVNC answered, and the first real session crashed the machine
it was watching.

@{b}Known:@{ub} on a PiStorm (Emu68, Picasso96) AmiVNC 1.0.0 ignores all pointer
input from any VNC client. The picture and keys work. Use the
@{" Screen window " link Screen} to click on that machine.

@endnode

@node ARexx "ARexx"

amifleet68's port is @{b}AMIFLEET.1@{ub}. Besides MUI's standard commands
(QUIT, HIDE, SHOW, ...):

  MACHINES                     one line per machine:
                               name state ms agent cpu os
  STATE NAME/A                 online | offline | token | busy | unknown
                               (RC 10: no such machine)
  POLL                         ask every machine now
  ADD NAME/A,HOST/A,PORT/N,TOKEN/K
  DETAILS NAME/A               open that window on a machine
  SCREEN NAME/A
  VNC NAME/A
  CONNECT, DISCONNECT          the VNC window's session

Example:

  /* who is up? */
  options results
  address 'AMIFLEET.1'
  'MACHINES'
  say RESULT

@endnode

@node Tips "Tips and limits"

  - Close the Screen window when you do not need it: every fetch keeps
    that machine's agent busy for a moment.
  - The agent runs one command at a time. A second Run on the same
    machine waits for the first.
  - Links show as drawers in the Files list: that is how the agent
    reports them.
  - The window layout is remembered by MUI. MUI Settings... in the
    Project menu adjusts fonts and looks.

@endnode

@node Security "Security"

amiagent runs any command for anyone who can reach its port and knows the
token. The token and all data travel unencrypted, VNC included.

  - Always start amiagent with a TOKEN.
  - Keep the fleet on a network you trust. Never forward port 7846 (or
    AmiVNC's 5900/5901) from the Internet.

amifleet68 stores the tokens in plain text in ENV: and ENVARC:.

@endnode

@node History "History"

@{b}0.3.0@{ub} (30.9.2026)  first public release
  - Screen window (agent HASH/SHOT) with click, drag and key forwarding
  - VNC client for AmiVNC, with start-through-the-agent and paced updates
  - Download and Upload in the Files page
  - ARexx commands, tooltips, this manual

@{b}0.1.0@{ub}  fleet board, details, shell with Break, file browser, LAN scan

@endnode

